---
title: "Dictionary encoding, one platform at a time"
author: bounty-hunter
date: 2026-11-19
abstract: A worked tour of stdlib/dict_encoding.ltl using six train departures. Learn how first appearance fixes dictionary order, how decode rebuilds a row, and exactly when four entries fit in two bits but five need three.
---

# Six trains, a handful of platforms

Picture a departure board with six rows. Each row names the platform the train leaves from:

```
Platform_A  Platform_B  Platform_A  Platform_C  Platform_B  Platform_A
```

The labels repeat. Storing the same strings over and over wastes space — the classic problem in columnar formats such as Parquet (PLAIN_DICTIONARY) and Arrow (DictionaryArray). **Dictionary encoding** replaces the label column with two smaller pieces:

- a **dictionary**: each distinct label, stored once;
- **indices**: one integer per row, pointing into the dictionary.

`stdlib/dict_encoding.ltl` gives us four functions: `encode`, `decode`, `index_bit_width`, and `cardinality`.

## encode: first appearance sets the order

The dictionary order is not alphabetical. It is the order in which labels are *first seen*:

```
encode(["Platform_A", "Platform_B", "Platform_A",
        "Platform_C", "Platform_B", "Platform_A"])
```

Walk the rows left to right. The first `Platform_A` is new, so it becomes index 0. `Platform_B` is new, index 1. The second `Platform_A` is already in the dictionary, so it reuses 0. `Platform_C` is new, index 2. The result:

```
dictionary = ["Platform_A", "Platform_B", "Platform_C"]
indices    = [0, 1, 0, 2, 1, 0]
```

Six labels collapsed into three strings and six tiny integers. The machinery behind it is a list for the dictionary and a map from label to index; a `while` loop appends a new entry only when `contains(dict_idx, k)` is false.

## decode: rebuild the original row

Decoding walks the indices and looks each one up in the dictionary:

```
decode(enc) == ["Platform_A", "Platform_B", "Platform_A",
                "Platform_C", "Platform_B", "Platform_A"]
```

Round-tripping is lossless: `decode(encode(values)) == values`, repeated labels and all.

## The bit-width boundary: four fit in two, five need three

Once the dictionary is shared once, each index only needs enough bits to name the largest entry. That is `ceil(log2(cardinality))`, returned by `index_bit_width`:

```
cardinality 4  ->  index_bit_width 2   (0..3 fit in two bits)
cardinality 5  ->  index_bit_width 3   (0..4 need three bits)
```

This is why a fourth dictionary entry is free (still two bits) but a fifth one bumps every index up to three bits. The function computes it with an integer shift loop — no floating point — and returns `1` for empty or single-entry columns so the result is always usable.

## Runnable example and pinned vectors

The companion file `examples/tutorials/dict_encoding_train.ltl` runs the departure-board example end to end:

```
$ ltlc run examples/tutorials/dict_encoding_train.ltl
dictionary : ['Platform_A', 'Platform_B', 'Platform_C']
indices    : [0, 1, 0, 2, 1, 0]
decoded    : ['Platform_A', 'Platform_B', 'Platform_A', 'Platform_C', 'Platform_B', 'Platform_A']
cardinality: 3
bits/value : 2
```

It ends with five pinned `@test` vectors, so `lateralus test` self-verifies the behaviour this tutorial describes:

1. repeated labels — dictionary order and index round-trip;
2. a one-entry column — all indices zero, bit width 1;
3. an empty column — empty dictionary, bit width 1;
4. four entries — the 2-bit boundary;
5. five entries — the 3-bit boundary.

## Why it matters

Dictionary encoding is the quiet workhorse behind analytic databases. Low-cardinality columns — platform names, country codes, status enums — shrink dramatically once the repeated strings are factored out. `stdlib/dict_encoding.ltl` brings that technique to Lateralus in a few dozen lines, fully testable with pinned vectors.

